<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="urea degradation II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5704" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Many organisms possess enzymes for the transformation of |FRAME: UREA| into |FRAME: AMMONIA| and |FRAME:CARBON-DIOXIDE|. This conversion can occur in two distinct routes, catalyzed by the enzymes |FRAME: CPLX-6897| and |FRAME:ENZRXN3O-319|, respectively. The nickel-enzyme |FRAME: EC-3.5.1.5|, catalyzes the ATP independent cleavage of the amide bonds of urea, forming |FRAME: AMMONIA| and |FRAME:CARBON-DIOXIDE| in a single step. Ureases have been described in plants, fungi, and bacteria, and have been extensively characterized. This enzyme is nickel dependent, and a number of accessory proteins are necessary for formation of the active enzyme |CITS:[12896998][17205208]|. The enzyme is particularly important for the human pathogen |FRAME: TAX-210|, which uses |FRAME: AMMONIA| production by urease for raising the pH in its microenvironment, as well as for controlling the pH homeostasis within the cell |CITS: [17464061]|. In that organism, urease accounts for 1015% of the total protein content of the cell |CITS: [9155571]|. The other route of urea conversion, catalyzed by |FRAME:ENZRXN3O-319|, has been reported for yeast, algae |CITS: [5679987][4556303][6753831]| and some bacteria |CITS: [15090492]|. It is described in |FRAME: PWY-5703|.</Description>
